# Test: a third-party plugin declares a pipe operation, and its handler receives it
#
# The one question: when an external plugin declares, at Stage 1, a `Pipes`
# entry with an `argument` field and no expansion on one of its own
# commands, is the operation recorded under the plugin, sent to its handler
# in the execute-command pipe list when an operator types it after a pipe,
# and listed by `show command help`? TestPluginPipeDeclWithArgumentIsAnOperation
# and TestRouteToProcessCarriesPipe prove the recording and the carrier; only
# a forked plugin proves the declaration and the list cross the process
# boundary.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-pipe-chain-honored-on-every-command-surface AC-14 (the
#   recording and the delivery), AC-19 (the plugin row), user story 5.
# PREVENTS: a plugin's operation recorded only for an internal plugin, or
#   folded into the handler's arguments, so a third-party command cannot
#   declare what follows its pipe.
#
# Fixture: internal/test/fixture plugin/pipe-operation-declared
# (plugin_fixture_pipe_operation.go, written in package 5a). Its contract,
# which this file asserts:
#   - Registration: one command `show fixture pipe-op` (YANG in the
#     fixture's module), Pipes = [{Name: "histogram", Argument: "none",
#     Description: "Count the rows of each length."}].
#   - OnExecuteCommand: answers done with data {"pipe-operations": [<name of
#     each operation received, in order>], "args": [<arguments received>]}.
#   - It returns an error, never an answer, when the list arrives empty for
#     a line that typed the operation, so the red names the missing list.
#
# Red before package 5a: Stage 1 refused a Pipes entry without an expansion
#   (validatePipeDecls), so the plugin failed to start. Seq 5 (the help row)
#   stays red until package 7a builds the pipe-operations list.
#
# Method: an external plugin forked from the config, `ze cli -c` over SSH as
# pipe-quoted-value-is-an-argument.ci does.

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ZE_CONFIG_DIR:value=.
option=env:var=XDG_DATA_HOME:value=.
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass

stdin=init-answers:terminator=EOF_INIT
admin
testpass
127.0.0.1
$PORT2
EOF_INIT

tmpfs=pipe-op.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		# bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
		user admin {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
		}
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
bus {
	endpoint pipe-operation-declared {
		external {
			run "le test fixture plugin/pipe-operation-declared";
		}
		encoding json;
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze init:stdin=init-answers:exit=0
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze start pipe-op.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.

# AC-14: the declared operation reaches the handler as an operation, not an
# argument, and the generic `json` after it stays the engine's.
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze cli -c "show fixture pipe-op | histogram | json":exit=0
# `| json` answers indented JSON, one array element per line, so the patterns
# allow any whitespace (newlines included) between the brackets.
expect=stdout:pattern="pipe-operations":\s*\[\s*"histogram"\s*\]
expect=stdout:pattern="args":\s*\[\s*\]
reject=stdout:contains="json"

# AC-13 on a plugin's set: an operation it does not declare is refused,
# naming the valid set, and the handler is not called.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "show fixture pipe-op | bogus"
expect=stderr:contains=histogram
reject=stdout:contains=pipe-operations

# AC-19, the plugin row, from the daemon: help lists the operation with its
# source and its own description.
cmd=foreground:seq=5:exec=ze cli -c 'show command help "show fixture pipe-op" | json':exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern="name": ?"histogram"
expect=stdout:contains=Count the rows of each length.

cmd=stop:seq=6:name=daemon:signal=term
